The reason for this is that Python's name-mangling rules replace the private name `__update` with `_Mapping__update` in the `Mapping` class and `_MappingSubclass__update` in the `MappingSubclass` class. When the `__helper` method in `MappingSubclass` tries to access the private `__update` method, it is actually trying to access the mangled name `_MappingSubclass__update`, not the original `_Mapping__update` method.

To fix this issue, the `__helper` method in `MappingSubclass` should use the mangled name of the private `__update` method, which is `_Mapping__update`. However, this is not the correct solution because it would break the encapsulation of the `Mapping` class.

